-- evaluator-speed (2026-11-16): why the machine ran at 1 to 5 million interaction steps a second
-- (lib/nbe-model.cub about 1.4M a second, lib/nbe.cub about 4.5M), what was fixed, and what remains,
-- with the evidence for each. How it was measured, so it can be repeated: the tree at a commit
-- unpacked outside the repository (git archive HEAD | tar -x -C DIR: another session edits the working
-- copy), the machine built twice from the sources before and after a change, each file run uncached
-- and on one worker (CUBICAL_NOCACHE=1 CUBICAL_WORKERS=0, --read), outputs compared byte for byte, and
-- the time measured with gprof from a build with -pg -static (static, so time spent in the C library,
-- string comparison and malloc, is attributed too); call counts of a C library function by its caller
-- came from a build whose strcmp was a macro counting by __func__.
-- Known (2026-11-16, at src/lang.c): fixed. Most of a run's time was not interactions: it was searches by
-- name, a string comparison per entry of a table that grows with everything loaded, so each run got
-- slower as the library grew. In lib/views.cub (203M steps) gprof gave 62% of the run (176 s of 281 s)
-- to lookup, the compiler's binder lookup, 21.6M calls each scanning the whole binder stack (thousands
-- of binders deep in a read-back term): compile called it three times for every application whose head
-- is not a variable (a definition, constructor or field: nearly every head), and lazy_trivial and
-- local_free once. They now call lookup_i, the epoch-cached lookup compile already used for
-- variables: it returns the same binder (the latest of the name; CUB_CHKBIND=1 checks every cached
-- answer against the scan), and every name reaching these sites is interned, as lookup_i needs.
-- lookup_i also read the environment (getenv("CUB_CHKBIND")) on every cached answer; it reads it once.
-- The remaining scans, counted in the same file: con_lookup's "%%c" (the checker's own constructor,
-- the first declared of that name) scanned every constructor, 18M lookups and 1.6 billion string
-- comparisons; the elaborator's imask_of and sig_of (implicit-argument masks, signatures) 1.1 billion
-- and 0.9 billion; data_lookup 0.7 billion; checked_index, called by the store's canonical form for
-- every symbol (7.3M times), each call a scan of thousands of entries; in lib/library.cub,
-- srcname 1.4 billion. Each is now an index from name to entry (NameIx in src/lang.c; CONF beside
-- con_lookup's CONH for the first of a name; CHKPREV linking a checked name's entries, so a hidden
-- entry is passed over as the scan passed over it). Each answers what its scan answered, the latest
-- entry of the name (the first for "%%c"; srcname's names are unique), because those tables are only
-- appended to and an entry's name never changes, except at #retract, which renames a data type's
-- constructors (con_lookup's tables were already rebuilt then) and the data type itself (the data
-- index is rebuilt then). lazy_trivial compared an interned head with eight literals by strcmp; it
-- compares pointers (IS), as compile does.
-- Known (2026-11-16, at src/net.c): fixed. synkey, the key of a compiled term in the frame build_m is
-- building, walked the whole subterm, and build_m calls it at every keyed node it builds, so each node
-- was walked once for itself and again for every keyed node above it: in lib/views.cub 133M calls and
-- 837M more recursive ones for 79M nodes built, and the keys those walks interned were freed and
-- interned again by the next walk (key interning, key_unlink and key_destroy, a ninth of the run). Now
-- run_frame gives the frame a table of synkey's results by node (KCache). It is exact:
-- synkey(t, f) is a function of t, the keys of the slots free in t and the frame's scrut; a slot is
-- bound (bind, bind_dim) before anything under its binder is built and is never bound again in the
-- frame, and every call of synkey on t comes after t is built, so those keys are fixed whenever synkey
-- is asked. The one exception is synkey's own reading of a let, which binds the let's slot to the key
-- of the let's value rather than of the built value: below it nothing is read from or written to the
-- table. The table's keys are released when the frame is done; keys are interned, so holding one longer
-- changes no key.
-- Results, every output identical (each passed and failed count, each step count, each #show line),
-- the tree at 13bd858, one worker, uncached: lib/views.cub (203M steps) 6 min 48 s to 1 min 43 s of
-- processor time, 0.50M to 1.97M steps a second; workflow/hyperactivity.cub (196M steps) 6 min 28 s to
-- 1 min 39 s; lib/library.cub (214M steps; 2511 passed, 24 failed before and after) 1 min 42 s to 48 s,
-- 2.1M to 4.4M steps a second; Brunerie (lib/core.cub with experiments/brunerie/typed.cub and
-- run-typed.cub, 8.7M steps) 8.8 s to 3.1 s, and the same value and steps on four workers;
-- experiments/brunerie/run-circle.cub, run-square.cub, every file of experiments/surface (failures
-- included), experiments/tso and experiments/obs identical. Not verified: lib/nbe.cub and
-- lib/nbe-model.cub, which the container's memory limit killed on both machines while another session's
-- machine ran (below).
-- Known (2026-11-16, at src/net.c): needs-work. Where the time goes now: gprof of lib/library.cub (41 s
-- sampled) has no function above 6%. The interactions themselves (interact, cell, link, link_now,
-- cell_free, copy_top, lazy_copy, flush_frees, retire_boxes, pop) about 13 s, some 60 ns an
-- interaction; building bodies (build_m, run_frame, synkey, interning keys, registering producers in
-- the memo) about 8 s; malloc, free, calloc and memset about 5.5 s; the front end (compile, lvs and
-- maxlev, intern, the lexer, syn_eq, SHA-256, rec_intern) about 8 s. What remains, by cause, each with
-- its fix and why the fix changes no result:
-- (1) Every keyed node built gets an interned key and every keyed producer a memo entry, whether or not
-- the memo ever shares its value: lib/views.cub began 71M memo producers (memo_begin) for 1.77M hits,
-- dropped 176M entries (memo_drop) and called key() 301M times, about 70 ns each, most of it a cache
-- miss in a key table of millions; each Entry is calloc'd and freed and each Key malloc'd and freed.
-- Fix: allocate Keys (by number of children) and Entries from per-thread free lists, as cells and
-- variables already are; only addresses change, and nothing observable depends on an address (keys
-- compare by identity; a hash shard is chosen from children's addresses, which only places the key).
-- (2) Leaf keys are interned again at every build: a word literal's (S_WORD, 32M in lib/views.cub), the
-- V_GLOBAL cell of every call (key_global from build_m, 39M), the head of every call's key. A key of a
-- global or a constructor with no children is immortal (KIMMORTAL), so a per-id array of them returns
-- the pointer interning returns; a word literal's key can be held by its compiled node and released
-- when the node is freed (syn_free).
-- (3) A thunk's key walks its whole record body in a frame of its own (52M node walks in
-- lib/views.cub), and the body is walked again when the thunk is built; a thunk inside a thunk's body
-- is walked once per enclosing thunk. The result is a function of the record and its environment's
-- keys, which key(K_LAM, record, environment) already names: kept on that key (a field of Key, released
-- with it) each body is walked once per environment.
-- (4) Memory: lib/nbe.cub was killed at 6.25 GB resident (after the fixes, 224 s in) and at 4.15 GB
-- (before them, 247 s in, another machine using 1.9 GB beside it) by the container's memory limit. The
-- peak is within single checks: between definitions (CUBICAL_MEMDEF=1, after dgdiag-nat) 35k cells and
-- 8k keys are live, malloc holds 509 MB in use and 832 MB free, 1.3 GB resident; the free part is
-- fragmentation, malloc'd keys, entries and boxes interleaved with the 64 KB cell slabs
-- (aligned_alloc). A keyed producer costs a cell (48 bytes and 8 a port), a variable per wire (24), a
-- key (40 and 8 a child, malloc'd) and an entry (40, calloc'd), and a shared value a box (56) with a
-- root cell. The pools of (1) take keys, entries and boxes out of malloc, so a check's peak is returned
-- to the slabs it came from. Which nbe definition peaks is the first measurement to make (CUBICAL_MEMDEF
-- prints after each definition; the run is to be made with nothing else running).
-- (5) Front end: compile compares a %-headed form's head with each name of four tables of primitives
-- (PO, PL, PR, TBN) by strcmp, 120M comparisons in lib/library.cub; is() compares a symbol with a
-- literal by strcmp when the pointers differ (46M); node_eq (35M) and vsrc (43M). An index by
-- interned name (NameIx over the primitive tables) answers as the scans do.
-- (6) Every build of the machine is a new checker identity (src/lang.c mixes __DATE__ and __TIME__ into
-- the store's keys), so any change to the machine re-verifies the whole store (94 s cold): measure
-- uncached, as above.
evaluator-speed : Node
evaluator-speed = node "The machine's time goes to the language's interactions: no search by name and no repeated walk grows with the program or the library." (cons language-implementation-remainder nil)
